osition DAG is a critical issue: the diagram misrepresents the DAG that will be executed. Correction: either add the E001 --> E006 edge to the diagram, or remove E001 from E006's `dependencies` (Rule 11 requires docs-authoring to be blocked by Implementation epics; the audit's effect on the documented posture is already transitive via E002/E003/E005) — whichever matches the intended gating semantics, applied consistently across diagram, justification table, and frontmatter.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: KServe/RHOAI Serving (E001, E002, E004, E005), Platform Operator/RHOAI Platform (E003), Documentation/RHOAI Documentation (E006); all component values are in the canonical list. E001 and E005 each span KServe, the serving data plane, and the Platform Operator in scope but are single logical changes (one audit pass; one evidence package per HLR-2) assigned the single most-relevant canonical component, with the span and the inferred team assignments explicitly flagged in the summary's Health Warnings — acceptable boundary handling, not bundling of different teams' work (the Platform Operator's engineering contribution lives in E003).\n\nNo scope duplication: E002/E003 split along the component axis, E001 produces the inventory that E005 consumes rather than rebuilding it, and E004's CI check is distinct from E001's audit method. No work-product type mixing: documentation authoring is isolated in E006; E002/E003's \"document the test results\" is evidence capture incidental to the code change, not content authoring.\n\nSize: E002's potential overage is acknowledged with a concrete re-split trigger recorded in the Health Warnings (\"re-split per image or per serving runtime if the audit finds materially more affected images or crypto libraries… or if remediation approaches diverge enough to require separate review cycles\") — a managed trade-off per the overage carve-out, not a boundary violation.\n\n### 4. Type Correctnes