{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fdc79d0deb2dce2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'E006 frontmatter lists RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 in dependencies and the\n10\t    DAG Justification table asserts (+E001) -> E006, but the mermaid diagram omits\n11\t    the E001 --> E006 edge (4 edges into E006 vs 5 frontmatter dependencies) — frontmatter\n12\t    dependencies inconsistent with the decomposition DAG diagram. Correction: add\n13\t    the E001 --> E006 edge to the diagram, or remove E001 from E006 dependencies (Rule\n14\t    11 requires docs-authoring blocked by Implementation epics; the audit is already\n15\t    transitive via E002/E003/E005) — applied consistently across diagram, justification\n16\t    table, and frontmatter.'\n17\t- severity: minor\n18\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n19\t  description: 'E006 (docs-authoring) lists Investigation E001 in dependencies with\n20\t    gated_by: null — an Investigation dependency missing gated_by (Criterion 4 band-1\n21\t    condition; execution unaffected because E006 is transitively gated via E002/E003/E005).\n22\t    Correction: set gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 with a gate_failure_impact (action\n23\t    + fallback_approach), or drop the redundant direct E001 dependency, consistent\n24\t    with the DAG Coherence fix.'\n25\terror: null\n26\t---\n27\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9013\n28\t\n29\t## Review Summary\n30\t\n31\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n32\tRecommendation: revise\n33\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — no VIOLATION lines; body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and re-split-trigger recording all pass). This review graded only what the validator cannot check.\n34\t\n35\tTriage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition) with 6 epics — multi-epic output is expected; no Step 0 contradiction.\n36\t\n37\tThe decomposition is otherwise strong: all four HLRs traced, correct handling of the docs-authoring priority exception, correct Rule 10 discipline (no konflux chain generated for existing components; build-pipeline coupling expressed as ACs), a recorded re-split trigger for the E002 size overage, and externally controlled schedule gates correctly kept out of acceptance criteria. One critical inconsistency in the DAG rendering and one gating-metadata defect force revision.\n38\t\n39\t## Criterion Details\n40\t\n41\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n42\tAll HLRs map to epics via a present traceability matrix: HLR-1 (P0) → E002, E003; HLR-2 (P0) → E001 (module inventory), E005 (package and submission); HLR-3 (P1) → E004; HLR-4 (P2) → E006 (covered, not deferred). Epic-level HLR Traceability sections match the matrix.\n43\t\n44\tPriority inheritance verified by enumerating every epic's dependents: E001 (P0) blocks P0 work — itself P0, correct. E002 (P0) and E003 (P0) block E005 (P0). E004 (P1) and E005 (P0) block only E006, a docs-authoring epic exempt from priority inheritance — no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work. E006's P0 label correctly derives from the strategy's Critical priority per the docs-authoring exception, not from HLR-4's P2. No epic maps HLRs at mixed priority levels, so no priority-collapse concern arises; HLR-3 and HLR-4 are correctly separated as deferrable features.\n45\t\n46\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n47\tNo cycles; all referenced epics exist; arrow direction is dependency → dependent; critical path E001 → E002 → E005 → E006 (length 4) is appropriate for a multi-component M strategy with no new components. Parallelism is correctly preserved: E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 after the shared audit, E004 ∥ E005, and no konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 pairings are vacuous. The E001 → E004 and E001 → E005 edges are legitimate: the CI check's detection logic builds against the audit's classification and the inventory is a required content element of the evidence package, and both carry proper `gated_by` metadata. The E002/E003 → E005 edges are justified by deliverable consumption (remediated images and FIPS-cluster test results — engineering deliverables, not merely build-pipeline outputs consumed as content), and E005 additionally carries the attestations-inclusion AC, so the Rule 13 content-consumption concern does not apply.\n48\t\n49\tHowever, the bidirectional diagram/frontmatter cross-check fails: **E006's frontmatter lists RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 in its `dependencies`, and the DAG Justification table asserts \"E002/E003/E004/E005 (+E001) → E006\", but the mermaid diagram omits the E001 --> E006 edge.** The diagram shows only four edges into E006 (from E002, E003, E004, E005) against five frontmatter dependencies. This is not a consistent transitive-reduction rendering — the diagram does draw other transitively implied edges (e.g., E002 --> E006 via E005) — so the summary's own diagram contradicts both the epic frontmatter and the summary's own justification table. Frontmatter dependencies inconsistent with the decomposition DAG is a critical issue: the diagram misrepresents the DAG that will be executed. Correction: either add the E001 --> E006 edge to the diagram, or remove E001 from E006's `dependencies` (Rule 11 requires docs-authoring to be blocked by Implementation epics; the audit's effect on the documented posture is already transitive via E002/E003/E005) — whichever matches the intended gating semantics, applied consistently across diagram, justification table, and frontmatter.\n50\t\n51\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n52\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: KServe/RHOAI Serving (E001, E002, E004, E005), Platform Operator/RHOAI Platform (E003), Documentation/RHOAI Documentation (E006); all component values are in the canonical list. E001 and E005 each span KServe, the serving data plane, and the Platform Operator in scope but are single logical changes (one audit pass; one evidence package per HLR-2) assigned the single most-relevant canonical component, with the span and the inferred team assignments explicitly flagged in the summary's Health Warnings — acceptable boundary handling, not bundling of different teams' work (the Platform Operator's engineering contribution lives in E003).\n53\t\n54\tNo scope duplication: E002/E003 split along the component axis, E001 produces the inventory that E005 consumes rather than rebuilding it, and E004's CI check is distinct from E001's audit method. No work-product type mixing: documentation authoring is isolated in E006; E002/E003's \"document the test results\" is evidence capture incidental to the code change, not content authoring.\n55\t\n56\tSize: E002's potential overage is acknowledged with a concrete re-split trigger recorded in the Health Warnings (\"re-split per image or per serving runtime if the audit finds materially more affected images or crypto libraries… or if remediation approaches diverge enough to require separate review cycles\") — a managed trade-off per the overage carve-out, not a boundary violation.\n57\t\n58\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n59\tTypes are correct: E001 is a genuine Investigation (its outcome rewrites the scope of E002/E003/E004/E005 — all four carry `gated_by: E001` with `rewrite` actions and populated fallbacks), and every Implementation epic produces a concrete artifact. E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) with `ai_signals: null`; E002–E006 carry `ai_signals` with `investigation_signals: null` — no rubric mismatch. Every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`.\n60\t\n61\tOne gating-metadata defect: **E006 (docs-authoring) lists Investigation E001 in its `dependencies` with `gated_by: null`.** Per the Criterion 4 band-1 rubric, an Investigation dependency missing `gated_by` on a downstream epic is a scoring condition. In substance the defect does not change execution — E006 is transitively gated on the audit via E002/E003/E005, and the docs epic's existence does not hinge on the audit outcome — so it is recorded as minor, but it should be fixed for automated gate detection: either set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9013-E001` with a `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach), or drop the redundant direct E001 dependency (consistent with the Criterion 2 correction).\n62\t\n63\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n64\tType-appropriate signal fields are present in every epic and every ai-signals rationale file exists. E001's investigation signals are justified (question_specificity 1 with a defined completion criterion; source_accessibility 1 from public images/repos and validated-module program records; local_runnability 1 for static inspection; cluster/hardware and human-judgment 0 — the team review in E001's AC is an engineering review, not an external decision gate).\n65\t\n66\tImplementation signals check out against the strategy: E002/E003 `open_questions: -1` is consistent with their audit gating; E005's `existing_foundation: -1` (strategy states no evidence package exists), `external_dependency: -1` (certification lab), and `human_process_gates: -1` (review board) all match the strategy text. Architecture-claims calibration verified against `.context`: only `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` exists; E002/E004/E005 score `architecture_claims: 1` citing it (Konflux-onboarded, midstream repos — both corroborated by the file), and E003/E006 score 0 because no architecture-context file covers Platform Operator or Documentation — accurate, not a calibration miss, and the gap is flagged in the summary. Computed `ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` values were not re-derived per the review contract.\n67\t\n68\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n69\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E006 has the \"technically reviewed against the implemented and validated posture\" AC (Rule 24), and E002/E003 carry \"builds green through the platform build pipeline\" ACs (Rule 25 discipline) even though no konflux-onboarding epic exists. E002/E003 are crypto remediation of existing images, not capability replacements, so Rule 23 rollback/feature-flag ACs are not triggered. Externally controlled conditions are correctly handled as risks, not ACs: lab turnaround and review-board cadence live in E005's Risks and Notes and the summary's Health Warnings, while E005's ACs stop at the engineering completion point (package assembled, sign-off obtained at defined milestones, submission made and triaged) — the sign-off AC states an outcome of the epic's own deliverable work mandated by HLR-2, and the timing risk is separately recorded. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by grep — all cross-references are by name).\n70\t\n71\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n72\tFull scope coverage: image remediation (E002/E003 across both affected components and the serving data plane), evidence package and submission (E001 + E005), CI regression gating (E004, covering all in-scope images including the data plane), documentation (E006). Out-of-scope statements (workbenches, pipelines, training, Common Criteria) are respected and explicitly fenced in E006's support-boundary content. Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the incompressible external schedule gates appear in E005's Risks and the summary's Health Warnings; the missing-teams/missing-detail gaps in the strategy are surfaced as Health Warnings with inferred teams flagged for verification. The audit's multi-component scope (KServe components, serving data plane, Platform Operator) is fully accounted for downstream (E002, E003, E004's coverage). No conditional branches are used, and none are required — the audit's outcomes vary scope within fixed epics (handled via `rewrite` gates) rather than selecting between bounded alternatives. No docs-authoring gap: HLR-4 requires documentation and E006 exists.\n73\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-955bde654b35688a","agent_id":"a9ae5ad5c90ec7192","session_id":"bb183815-0547-4989-a1f9-5b702edd86b1","uuid":"1067f0ab-f9d1-40a6-9ec9-3d724e48a0db","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:03:20.022Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fdc79d0deb2dce2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
