{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90417023e6c72cc8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. Not triaged by Step 0: the\n13\tstrategy spans multiple components (KServe, Platform Operator) and multiple\n14\tarchitecturally distinct work streams (image remediation, CI gating, compliance\n15\tevidence assembly with external submission, documentation), so both\n16\tbelow-threshold escape conditions rule out a single-epic result. Check 2\n17\t(docs-only) does not apply — active code/build changes are required.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n22\t|-------|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | Cryptographic module audit of the serving stack | Investigation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS remediation of KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Platform Operator FIPS validation and remediation | Implementation | Platform Operator | RHOAI Platform (inferred) | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | CI gating check for FIPS crypto regressions | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 | Document the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation (inferred) | P0 |\n29\t\n30\tNotes on epic-list fields:\n31\t\n32\t- **Priority of the docs epic**: per the docs-authoring priority exception, the\n33\t  documentation epic's priority is derived from the strategy frontmatter\n34\t  priority (Critical → P0), not inherited from or pushed onto the\n35\t  implementations it depends on. HLR-4 itself is P2; the epic-level P0 label\n36\t  reflects strategy-level criticality only.\n37\t- **Team assignments**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see Health\n38\t  Warnings). RHOAI Serving for KServe comes from the KServe architecture\n39\t  context file; the Platform Operator and Documentation team assignments are\n40\t  inferred and flagged for verification.\n41\t- **Component span**: the crypto module audit (E001) and the evidence package\n42\t  (E005) span KServe, the serving data plane, and the Platform Operator; each\n43\t  is assigned the single most-relevant canonical component (KServe) per the\n44\t  component-name constraint.\n45\t\n46\t## Dependency DAG\n47\t\n48\t```mermaid\n49\tgraph TD\n50\t    E001[\"E001 Crypto module audit (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 KServe image FIPS remediation\"]\n51\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Platform Operator FIPS remediation\"]\n52\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 CI FIPS regression gate\"]\n53\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 Compliance evidence package\"]\n54\t    E002 --> E005\n55\t    E003 --> E005\n56\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 FIPS documentation (docs-authoring)\"]\n57\t    E003 --> E006\n58\t    E004 --> E006\n59\t    E005 --> E006\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 → E006 (4 epics).\n63\t\n64\t## DAG Justification\n65\t\n66\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n67\t|------|------|-----------|\n68\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The audit determines which images link non-validated crypto and the remediation approach per image; the remediation epic's scope is rewritten by the outcome. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n69\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | Same audit gates the Platform Operator work: if the operator image is already clean, that epic reduces to validation-only. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n70\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The CI check's detection logic builds against the audit's module inventory (the validated/non-validated classification that defines what the check fails on). |\n71\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 13 | The module inventory is a required content element of the evidence package (HLR-2); it is the audit's deliverable. |\n72\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 13 (deliverable consumption) | The evidence package incorporates the rebuilt images' build attestations and FIPS-cluster test results — E005's deliverable cannot exist without them. |\n73\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 13 (deliverable consumption) | Same for the Platform Operator's validation/remediation results. |\n74\t| E004 ∥ E005 | Rule 14 | Different deliverables (CI config vs. evidence document), no shared build artifact — parallel. |\n75\t| E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 | Rule 14 | Different components/repos and work products after the shared audit — parallel. |\n76\t| E002/E003/E004/E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. The audit's grounding of the documented posture is transitive via the remediation, CI, and evidence epics (each of which is gated on the audit), so no direct edge from the Investigation epic is drawn. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring exception). |\n77\t\n78\tNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are\n79\tgenerated: both affected components are existing platform components, and the\n80\tKServe architecture context records the component as already Konflux-onboarded.\n81\tBuild-pipeline coupling for the image-rebuild epics is expressed as\n82\t\"build pipeline green\" acceptance criteria, not DAG edges (Rule 10 discipline).\n83\t\n84\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n85\t\n86\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic(s) |\n87\t|-----|----------|------------------------|---------|\n88\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules | E002, E003 |\n89\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Compliance evidence package (module inventory, build attestations, test results) assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab | E001 (module inventory), E005 (package assembly and submission) |\n90\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI gating check fails builds reintroducing non-validated crypto usage | E004 |\n91\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | E006 |\n92\t\n93\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No epic maps HLRs at\n94\tmixed priority levels, so no priority-split or bundling decisions arise.\n95\tHLR-3 (P1) and HLR-4 (P2) are distinct, deferrable features with independent\n96\tuser value and separate surfaces from the P0 work, so they are separate epics.\n97\t\n98\t## Health Warnings\n99\t\n100\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking)**: the strategy has no Impacted\n101\t  Teams table, no per-component change/owner detail (Affected Components is a\n102\t  bare list), no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n103\t  and no Non-Functional Requirements. The decomposition proceeds from the\n104\t  HLRs and problem statement; team assignments for Platform Operator\n105\t  (\"RHOAI Platform\") and Documentation (\"RHOAI Documentation\") are inferred\n106\t  and should be verified with the component owners before scheduling.\n107\t- **Externally controlled schedule gates**: certification-lab turnaround\n108\t  (historically six to nine months) and security review board cadence are\n109\t  third-party/human gates the strategy itself flags as incompressible. They\n110\t  are recorded as risks on the evidence-package epic and a schedule warning\n111\t  here — not as acceptance criteria, which are written at the engineering\n112\t  completion point the strategy controls (package assembled, review-board\n113\t  sign-off obtained at defined milestones, submission made and triaged).\n114\t- **Architecture-context gap for Platform Operator**: no architecture-context\n115\t  file exists for Platform Operator, though it is a canonical RHAI Jira\n116\t  component. It is treated as an existing platform component (no onboarding\n117\t  chain generated — the strategy is about FIPS posture of existing images,\n118\t  not provisioning), and its epic's architecture-claims signal is scored 0\n119\t  for lack of corroboration. The context gap itself should be closed upstream.\n120\t- **Epic size (E002)**: the KServe image remediation epic is deliberately kept\n121\t  as one logical change (a single remediation pass over the serving-stack\n122\t  images defined by the audit) and may sit above the ~2-week epic guideline\n123\t  depending on audit findings. Re-split trigger: re-split E002 per image or\n124\t  per serving runtime if the audit finds materially more affected images or\n125\t  crypto libraries than estimated, or if remediation approaches diverge\n126\t  enough to require separate review cycles.\n127\t\n128\t## Tiered Delivery\n129\t\n130\tNot applicable as a split. The only external dependency with uncertain timing\n131\tis the certification lab, and no engineering work is gated on the lab: all\n132\tremediation, CI, and documentation work delivers independent partial value\n133\t(Tier 1-equivalent), while the lab-facing step is the terminal acceptance\n134\tpoint of the evidence-package epic (submission and triage), not a body of\n135\tdependency-gated engineering work. Lab certification outcome timing is\n136\tcarried as the schedule risk above rather than as a DAG tier boundary.\n137\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bacfe8ea362d9293","agent_id":"ac3468000e703d62b","session_id":"bb183815-0547-4989-a1f9-5b702edd86b1","uuid":"500f6318-e5e2-44c9-a50a-aec9ec46ec5e","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:05:34.564Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-90417023e6c72cc8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reas